(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ent-pathophysiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"ENT Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the characteristic frequency pattern of presbycusis, and why does it impair conversation?","choices":["Low frequencies go first, taking the vowels","High frequencies go first, taking the speech consonants","All frequencies fall equally","Only frequencies above the speech range are affected"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Consonants such as /s/, /f/ and /t/ are high-frequency sounds and carry most of the discriminating information in speech, which is why voices become hard to make out in a noisy room while still being audible. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which cytokines drive nasal polyp formation, and which cell floods the tissue?","choices":["Interleukins 4, 5 and 13, with eosinophil influx","Interleukins 1, 6 and tumour necrosis factor, with neutrophil influx","Interferon gamma, with lymphocyte influx","Interleukin 17, with mast cell influx"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is type 2 inflammation, and naming the three interleukins is the molecular-mechanism objective in action. Polyps are benign, non-neoplastic oedematous masses from the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which physical law explains why a small septal deviation causes disproportionate obstruction?","choices":["Poiseuille's law","Fick's law of diffusion","Laplace's law","Boyle's law"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resistance rises with the fourth power of the radius, so a modest narrowing has an outsized effect on airflow. That is why a deviation that looks minor can produce persistent obstruction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How are acute and chronic rhinosinusitis defined by duration, and what commonly accompanies the chronic form?","choices":["Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks despite therapy, often with nasal polyps","Acute under 2 weeks; chronic beyond 6 weeks, often with septal deviation","Acute under 12 weeks; chronic beyond 6 months, often with turbinate hypertrophy","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks, usually with no mucosal change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The two thresholds are the definition. Acute disease is usually viral &mdash; rhinovirus or influenza &mdash; with secondary bacterial infection by Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which organisms cause 80 to 90 per cent of otitis externa, and what predisposes to the fungal form?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae; fungal disease follows swimming","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus; fungal after antibiotics","Moraxella catarrhalis alone; fungal disease follows trauma","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus; fungal disease follows steroid use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial pair dominate, and Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans take over when the bacterial flora has been suppressed or the canal stays wet. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In conductive hearing loss, which way does Weber lateralise and what does Rinne show?","choices":["Weber to the AFFECTED ear; Rinne shows bone conduction greater than air","Weber to the UNAFFECTED ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber to the affected ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber is equal; Rinne shows bone greater than air bilaterally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The blocked ear is not receiving competing air-borne sound, so bone-conducted sound is heard more loudly in it — and with the conducting chain impaired, bone beats air on Rinne. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How do cisplatin and carboplatin cause permanent bilateral sensorineural loss?","choices":["They generate reactive oxygen species that destroy outer hair cells at the cochlear base","They cross-link DNA in stria vascularis cells, compromising endolymph ion homeostasis","They inhibit prestin in outer hair cells","They fix the stapes footplate in the oval window"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The stria vascularis maintains the ionic composition of endolymph, and without that gradient transduction fails. Damage there is permanent and bilateral. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two ototoxic effects are described as reversible or symptom-limited rather than permanent?","choices":["Gentamicin and tobramycin damage","Cisplatin and carboplatin damage","Furosemide on the strial potential; salicylate on prestin","Noise trauma and presbycusis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The loop diuretic effect on the strial potential is reversible, and the salicylate effect on the outer hair cell motor protein produces tinnitus. Neither destroys the cells outright. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How do the causes of anterior and posterior epistaxis differ?","choices":["Anterior from hypertension and anticoagulation; posterior from digital trauma","Both are caused predominantly by anticoagulation","Anterior from local trauma and dry air; posterior from hypertension and anticoagulation","Anterior from coagulopathy; posterior from low humidity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anterior bleeds are local and mechanical; posterior bleeds reflect a systemic vascular or clotting problem, which is also why they are more profuse and carry airway risk. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What happens to the wider nasal cavity opposite a septal deviation?","choices":["It atrophies from disuse","It develops polyps from the increased flow","Nothing changes; only the narrow side is affected","Compensatory inferior turbinate hypertrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The wide side does more of the work, so its turbinate enlarges to condition the extra airflow &mdash; which can then narrow the only good airway the patient had. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial otitis media?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans","Group A Streptococcus alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The classic middle ear trio. Viral causes are respiratory syncytial virus, rhinovirus, influenza and adenovirus. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the bone remodelling sequence in otosclerosis?","choices":["Osteoblastic overgrowth of dense cortical bone without resorption","Osteoclastic resorption, then spongy osteoid replacement","Demineralisation of the ossicles from chronic inflammation","Fibrous scarring of the round window membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bone is removed and replaced by soft vascular osteoid around the otic capsule and stapes footplate. The remodelled bone then fixes the footplate. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which Eustachian tube dysfunction creates negative middle ear pressure?","choices":["Mucus production increases faster than the tube can drain it","Bacterial infection erodes the tube lining and opens it permanently","The tube fails to open, so the mucosa absorbs the gases","The tympanic membrane becomes stiff and pulls the ossicles inward"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The middle ear is an air-filled space; if it is not re-ventilated, the mucosa absorbs the gases and the pressure falls. That negative pressure is the start of the otitis media sequence. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is canalithiasis?","choices":["Calcification of the stapes footplate","Dislodged otoconia floating free in the semicircular canals","Accumulation of endolymph in the membranous labyrinth","Inflammatory debris within the cochlea after infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The crystals normally sit in the utricle. Once loose in the endolymph they strike structures and generate a false sensation of motion. Why they dislodge is not the examinable part &mdash; that they can is. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which structures detect linear acceleration and gravitational position?","choices":["The otolith organs","The three semicircular canals","The cupula and ampulla","The organ of Corti"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They handle static and linear balance, while the semicircular canals handle rotation. The two systems together give the brain a complete picture of head position and movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the classic symptom tetrad of Meniere disease?","choices":["Continuous vertigo lasting days, unilateral tinnitus, unilateral loss, and nystagmus","Vertigo lasting under a minute on head movement, with no hearing loss","High-frequency hearing loss with tinnitus and no vertigo","Episodic vertigo for hours, low-frequency tinnitus, low-tone loss, aural fullness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four, and the LOW-frequency emphasis is what distinguishes the hearing loss from presbycusis or noise damage, which take the high frequencies first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which feature of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo separates it most cleanly from the other peripheral causes?","choices":["The vertigo lasts several hours","It follows a viral upper respiratory infection","There is NO hearing loss","It produces aural fullness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meniere disease and labyrinthitis both take hearing with them; this does not, because the otoconia are a vestibular problem that never touches the cochlea. Attacks also last under a minute and follow head position. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How do gentamicin and tobramycin damage hearing, and which frequencies go first?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in the stria vascularis, disturbing endolymph ion homeostasis","They alter the stria vascularis potential reversibly","They inhibit the prestin motor protein in outer hair cells","Reactive oxygen species destroy outer hair cells from the base, so high pitches go first"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The base of the cochlea encodes high frequencies, so basal damage shows as high-frequency loss. The mechanism is oxidative injury, not a direct toxic binding. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Cerumen impaction, foreign bodies and canal exostoses all cause conductive loss by which of the four core mechanisms?","choices":["Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity","Obstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A physical blockage stops sound penetrating down the canal. It is the first of four mechanisms, and the only one that acts before the sound reaches the drum. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Is the vertigo of labyrinthitis episodic or continuous, and how long does it run?","choices":["Episodic, with attacks lasting several hours","Episodic, with attacks lasting under a minute","Continuous, over days, improving across weeks","Continuous, permanent and non-improving"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Someone comes in saying the room has been spinning for three days, not that it comes and goes. Continuous vertigo that slowly settles over weeks is the labyrinthitis pattern, because the body is resolving an inflammatory process. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17 (slide says episodic; corrected in lecture)"},{"q":"In the organ of Corti, what physically opens the tip-link channels on the hair cells?","choices":["Direct pressure of endolymph on the hair cell body","Glutamate binding to the hair cell","Shearing of the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane","Stapes vibration transmitted directly to the hair cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Basilar membrane displacement bends the stereocilia against the rigid tectorial membrane, and that mechanical deflection is what opens the channels. Everything upstream exists to produce that shear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as PERIPHERAL rather than central?","choices":["Sudden onset, horizontal or rotational nystagmus, fatigable, suppressed by visual fixation","Gradual onset, vertical nystagmus, non-suppressible, with neurological deficits","Gradual onset with hearing loss and tinnitus","Absence of nystagmus entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four point at the inner ear or cranial nerve VIII. Suppression by visual fixation is the one most easily tested at the bedside. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does a deviated septum cause epistaxis and loss of smell?","choices":["Compression of the ethmoid arteries and the olfactory nerve","Chronic infection eroding the septal vessels and olfactory epithelium","Autoimmune destruction of the septal cartilage","Turbulent airflow dries the mucosa; air misses the cribriform plate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two separate consequences of the same disturbed airflow: local drying with fragile vessel breakdown on one hand, and failure to deliver odorant to the superior nasal vault on the other. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
